Project 75: AI-Based CT-to-Mesh Optimization for Simulation
1. Project title AI-Based CT-to-Mesh Optimization for Simulation
2. Background and problem statement Preparing a CT-derived mesh for FE/CFD simulation is a multi-stage pipeline — segmentation, surface extraction,
tetrahedralisation, and quality repair/smoothing — in which errors compound: a noisy segmentation yields a rough surface, which yields poor-quality tetrahedra,
which can crash a solver at assembly or convergence time. Manually iterating through the individual open-source tools (PyVista, trimesh, Open3D, TetGen/Gmsh) to
fix each stage in turn is slow and ad hoc, and a student project attempting to invent a wholly new meshing algorithm to fix this is out of scope for a 35-mark module.
This capstone instead builds an integrated, end-to-end pipeline that chains these existing open-source tools together and inserts exactly one learned component — a
lightweight mesh-quality-prediction/smoothing-guidance GNN, in the spirit of Project 74 — that automatically flags and locally corrects simulation-readiness
problems, and rigorously measures whether that single learned component improves simulation-readiness over the classical pipeline run with no correction at all.
3. Main objective To implement and evaluate an integrated CT-to-simulation-ready-mesh pipeline — segmentation-quality-aware surface extraction, TetGen/Gmsh
tetrahedralisation, and a single learned mesh-quality-driven local remeshing/smoothing correction stage — that measurably improves simulation-readiness
(poor/inverted-element rate, surface accuracy, volume preservation, and downstream FE-assembly success) over the same classical pipeline run without the learned
correction stage.
4. Specific tasks to be completed 1. Build a library of synthetic CT-like phantom volumes spanning a spread of segmentation-quality conditions (clean, moderately
degraded, poorly thresholded) to represent realistic imperfect student-facing CT preprocessing outcomes. 2. Implement the classical baseline pipeline end-to-end:
threshold segmentation → Marching Cubes surface (scikit-image) → surface repair (trimesh: fill holes, remove degenerate/non-manifold faces, fix normals) →
TetGen/Gmsh tetrahedralisation with default/uniform settings. 3. Train a lightweight GNN quality-prediction/smoothing-guidance model (a simplified, single-
purpose adaptation of the Project-74-style approach) that flags per-element “needs local correction” and suggests a smoothing-iteration weight, from local mesh-
quality features. 4. Integrate the learned component as a post-tetrahedralisation correction stage: identify flagged poor-quality regions, extract local patches
(PyVista/trimesh), and apply targeted local Laplacian/Taubin smoothing or a local TetGen re-mesh with tightened sizing restricted to those regions only. 5. Quantify
simulation-readiness before and after correction: poor/inverted-element fraction, surface Hausdorff distance to the known reference geometry, volume-
preservation error, and watertightness. 6. Run a lightweight downstream sanity check — assembling a simple FE stiffness/Laplacian system or a basic steady-state
heat-conduction solve (via scikit-fem or a minimal custom FE assembly script) on the before/after meshes — to demonstrate whether solver assembly succeeds and
how convergence behaviour differs, as a practical simulation-readiness proxy (a full CFD/FE simulation study is explicitly out of scope). 7. Benchmark end-to-end
pipeline runtime for the classical-only pipeline versus the classical-plus-learned-correction pipeline. 8. Critically discuss where the learned correction stage helps
most (e.g. thin/sharp-feature regions, poorly segmented inputs) versus where it offers little benefit over a simple non-learned global fix.
5. Required dataset/data A synthetic library of degraded CT-like phantom volumes (with a known clean reference mesh for each) generated entirely by the student’s
own procedural phantom pipeline; every stage of the pipeline is built on open-source tools operating on this synthetic data, with no dependency on a real CT
scanner.

6. Number of images/volumes/samples recommended 100-150 phantom instances spanning three segmentation-quality tiers (clean/moderate/poor), split
70/15/15, each carried through the full pipeline to produce a before/after mesh pair for evaluation.
7. Data format required .raw/.npy (input volumes); .stl (extracted surfaces); .vtu/.msh (tetrahedral meshes, before and after correction); .npz (GNN
features/predictions); FE-assembly logs as .json/.csv.
8. How the data can be collected or synthetically generated Phantoms are generated with the procedural CSG pipeline used elsewhere in this category
(independently instantiated, including thin ribs, fillets, and internal channels to stress simulation-readiness) and voxelised at a fixed resolution (e.g. 128³); the three
segmentation-quality tiers are produced by applying controlled degradation — Gaussian volume smoothing plus additive noise followed by re-thresholding at a
deliberately imperfect level — to the clean high-resolution mask, giving a fully controlled, reproducible spread of realistic imperfect-segmentation inputs without
needing a real scanner.
9. Ground-truth/label generation method All quality metrics used throughout the pipeline (scaled Jacobian, aspect ratio, dihedral angle) are computed by direct
deterministic geometric formulas, exactly as in Projects 73/74, requiring no manual annotation; the geometric “reference” against which Hausdorff distance and
volume-preservation error are measured is the known clean high-resolution phantom mesh generated before degradation was applied, giving an exact end-to-end
accuracy ground truth for the whole pipeline’s output.
10. Data preprocessing Resample/normalise volumes to a consistent voxel spacing; apply morphological cleanup (remove small floating connected components)
before surface extraction; run trimesh mesh-repair routines (fill_holes, remove_degenerate_faces, fix_normals) on the extracted surface prior to
tetrahedralisation to ensure a valid manifold input to TetGen/Gmsh.
11. Recommended AI/ML algorithms A single lightweight GraphSAGE-style GNN (as in Project 74, simplified) providing the pipeline’s one learned component: per-
element “needs correction” flagging and smoothing-weight suggestion; no other new model architecture is introduced — this project’s contribution is the pipeline
integration and evaluation, not new model design.
12. Proposed model architecture 3-layer GraphSAGE, hidden dimension 64, operating on the element-adjacency graph, output: a per-element binary “flag for local
correction” head plus a continuous suggested smoothing-iteration-weight head (0-1); approximately 80k parameters, deliberately kept small since it is one
component of a larger integrated system rather than the project’s research focus.
13. Complete project workflow 1. Generate phantom volumes across the three segmentation-quality tiers, retaining the clean reference mesh for each. 2.
Threshold-segment the degraded volume and extract a surface via Marching Cubes. 3. Repair the surface mesh with trimesh. 4. Tetrahedralise with TetGen/Gmsh at
default settings (classical-only checkpoint). 5. Compute per-element quality via PyVista/VTK cell-quality filters. 6. Run the trained GNN to flag poor-quality regions
and suggest smoothing weights. 7. Apply targeted local smoothing/re-meshing to flagged regions only (corrected checkpoint). 8. Re-verify mesh quality on the
corrected checkpoint. 9. Compute geometric accuracy (Hausdorff/volume error) of both checkpoints against the known clean reference mesh. 10. Run the FE/heat-
conduction assembly sanity check on both checkpoints. 11. Benchmark runtime and summarise results in a comparative report.
14. Training procedure The GNN component is trained exactly as in a Project-74-style setup: combined MSE (quality-score regression) + focal-loss (poor/inverted
classification), Adam, learning rate 1e-3, ~150 epochs, on a subset of tetrahedral meshes generated across the segmentation-quality spread, with rotation/rescaling
augmentation of the underlying phantom shapes; trainable on a single GPU given the model’s small size.
15. Testing procedure 70/15/15 split by phantom instance, with each split containing examples from all three segmentation-quality tiers; the full pipeline is
evaluated end-to-end only on the held-out test split, comparing the classical-only pipeline output against the classical-plus-learned-correction pipeline output on
identical input volumes.

16. Expected outputs Trained GNN weights; before/after mesh pairs for every test volume; quality-improvement summary tables and plots; FE-assembly
success/failure logs; a runtime-benchmark table; the full end-to-end pipeline code/notebook.
17. Evaluation metrics Poor/inverted-element fraction (before vs after correction); mean/minimum scaled Jacobian; surface Hausdorff distance to the known
reference mesh; volume-preservation error (%); watertightness rate; FE/heat-conduction assembly success rate and a solver-readiness proxy (condition number or
convergence-iteration count); end-to-end wall-clock runtime.
18. Minimum experiments required 1. Classical-only pipeline vs classical-plus-learned-correction pipeline across the full test set (primary comparison). 2. Results
stratified by input segmentation-quality tier (clean/moderate/poor) to identify where correction matters most. 3. Learned, targeted local correction vs a simple non-
learned baseline correction (uniform global Taubin smoothing plus uniform finer-global-size TetGen re-tetrahedralisation) — isolating the value of the learned,
spatially-targeted aspect specifically. 4. Ablation checking whether correction actually changes FE/heat-conduction assembly outcomes, not merely geometric
quality-metric values. 5. Runtime-overhead analysis of the learned-correction stage relative to total pipeline time.
19. Suggested comparison/baseline methods The full classical pipeline with no learned correction (thresholding → Marching Cubes → trimesh repair →
TetGen/Gmsh default tetrahedralisation only) as the primary baseline; a second baseline of “blind” uniform global smoothing/re-meshing applied everywhere (no
targeting) to isolate the specific value of learned, spatially-targeted correction.
20. Expected results A 25-45% reduction in the poor/inverted-element fraction after learned-targeted correction relative to the classical-only baseline, concentrated
on the “poor segmentation” input tier; FE/heat-conduction assembly success rate improving from roughly 70-85% (classical-only, poor tier) to ≥95% (with
correction); learned-targeted correction achieving quality improvement comparable to or better than blind uniform correction while touching substantially fewer
elements, at correspondingly lower runtime overhead.
21. Possible challenges Integrating multiple third-party meshing libraries whose Python APIs and versions can be fragile together (TetGen wrapper, Gmsh, trimesh,
PyVista); defining local re-meshing on flagged regions robustly without introducing new non-manifold boundaries at patch edges; keeping the FE sanity-check stage
genuinely minimal to avoid scope creep into a full CFD/FE simulation project; ensuring the classical-only vs corrected comparison is fair (matched element-count
budgets, matched runtime where relevant); reproducibility across the many chained open-source tools and their version dependencies.
22. Suggested Python libraries/software PyTorch, PyTorch Geometric, PyVista, VTK, trimesh, Open3D, scikit-image, tetgen/pygmsh/gmsh, scikit-fem (or a
minimal custom FE assembly script) for the sanity-check solve, NumPy, SciPy.
23. 35-mark evaluation scheme Literature review/background: 4; Data preparation: 5; Methodology/implementation: 11; Results &amp; evaluation: 8; Report &amp;
presentation: 4; Critical discussion: 3. (Total: 35)
24. Expected final deliverables Full modular pipeline code repository (segmentation → surface → tetrahedralisation → learned correction → FE sanity check);
trained GNN weights; the before/after mesh dataset; FE-sanity-check scripts and logs; a comparative report with plots/tables; presentation; a live demo running the
full pipeline end-to-end on 2-3 example volumes.
25. Difficulty level Research-oriented
26. Estimated completion time 14 weeks
27. Whether synthetic CT data, experimental CT data, or both should be used Synthetic only. A controlled, exact-ground-truth spectrum of segmentation-quality
degradation is required to isolate and attribute failures to specific pipeline stages reproducibly; the project is explicitly scoped as gluing together existing open-
source meshing tools plus one learned component, not inventing a new meshing algorithm, and no CT scanner access is assumed or required.